+# CSV import of contacts
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets. They want to load a whole CSV file into
+contactbook instead of adding contacts one by one, and they want a report of
+what happened: which contacts were created, which were updated, and which rows
+had problems, with row numbers they can find in their spreadsheet.
+
+The file has the same columns as our CSV export (`name,email,phone,tags`).
+Importing must never lose data we already hold. It merges into existing
+contacts and never deletes contacts, removes tags or clears phones.
+
+## Interface
+
+The interface is fixed. Other code calls exactly this:
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Definitions
+
+- **Row number**: the spreadsheet row number. The header is row 1 and the first row
+  after it is row 2. Every row counts, blank rows included. A quoted cell that
+  contains a line break does not start a new row: one CSV record is one row.
+- **Blank row**: a row where every cell is empty or only whitespace, such as an empty
+  line or the `,,,` lines Excel leaves at the bottom.
+- **Contact row**: any row after the header that is not blank.
+- **Applied row**: a contact row that ended up creating or updating a contact. Every
+  contact row is either applied or an error row.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Input and encoding
+
+1. `data` may be `bytes` or `str`. Any other type raises `TypeError`, not
+   `ImportFileError`.
+2. `bytes` are decoded as UTF-8. A leading UTF-8 BOM (Excel's "CSV UTF-8") is
+   accepted and is not part of the first column name.
+3. `str` input that starts with the BOM character `﻿` is handled the same way:
+   the BOM is not part of the first column name.
+4. If `bytes` are not valid UTF-8 (e.g. a Windows-1251 save), `ImportFileError` is
+   raised. Its message tells the user to re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8".
+5. Only comma-delimited files are supported. Standard CSV quoting, as produced by
+   `export_csv`, is understood: quoted cells, embedded commas, doubled quotes and
+   embedded line breaks.
+
+### Header and columns
+
+6. The first row of the file is the header. Column names are matched
+   case-insensitively and with surrounding whitespace ignored. Column order does
+   not matter.
+7. The `email` column is required. If it is missing, `ImportFileError` is raised.
+8. A name source is required. It is either a `name` column or both a `first_name`
+   and a `last_name` column.
+   - If `name` is present, it is used, and `first_name`/`last_name` are treated
+     as unknown columns.
+   - If `name` is absent and both `first_name` and `last_name` are present, the
+     name is the first-name cell and the last-name cell joined by one space. A
+     blank part leaves just the other part, e.g. `Ann` + `` → `Ann`.
+   - If `name` is absent and fewer than both of `first_name`/`last_name` are present,
+     `ImportFileError` is raised.
+9. `phone` and `tags` are optional columns. A missing one behaves as if every cell
+   in it were blank.
+10. Unknown columns (e.g. `Company`, `Notes`) are ignored. They never cause an error
+    or a warning.
+11. If a column that is used appears more than once in the header (after
+    case/whitespace matching, e.g. `Email` and ` email`), `ImportFileError` is
+    raised. Repeated unknown columns are fine.
+12. A completely empty file raises `ImportFileError`. This includes zero bytes, only a
+    BOM, or only whitespace.
+13. A file with only a header, or a header followed only by blank rows, imports
+    nothing. It returns a report with all four lists empty.
+14. A row with fewer cells than the header has its missing cells treated as blank.
+    Cells beyond the header are ignored. Neither one rejects the file.
+
+### Size limit
+
+15. A file with more than 1000 contact rows raises `ImportFileError` and imports
+    nothing. Exactly 1000 contact rows is accepted. Blank rows don't count toward
+    the limit. Error rows do count.
+
+### Whole-file failures
+
+16. Whenever `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is left exactly as it was:
+    no contact is added or changed.
+
+### Per-row validation
+
+17. **Email**: normalised with the existing email helper (trim + lower-case). A
+    missing or invalid email makes the row an error with column `email`.
+18. **Name**: required on every contact row, including rows that update an existing
+    contact. It is validated with the existing name helper. A blank or invalid name
+    makes the row an error with column `name`. This also applies in
+    `first_name`/`last_name` files, where both cells blank is a `name` error.
+19. **Tags**: the cell holds tags separated by `;`. Each piece is normalised with the
+    existing tag helper. Empty pieces and repeats are ignored. An invalid tag makes
+    the row an error with column `tags`. A blank cell means no tags.
+20. **Phone**: a blank cell means "no phone given". A valid phone is normalised with
+    the existing phone helper. An invalid phone does **not** make the row an error.
+    The row is still applied, the phone is left as it was (no phone for a new
+    contact), and one warning is added for that row with column `phone`.
+21. **Duplicate email in the file**: if a contact row's normalised email already
+    appeared on an earlier contact row of the same file, the later row is an error
+    with column `email` and is not applied. This holds whether or not the earlier
+    row was applied. The first occurrence is processed normally.
+
+### Error rows
+
+22. An error row changes nothing in the repository. It is not even partly applied
+    to an existing contact.
+23. Each error row produces at least one entry in `errors` with that row's number.
+    An error row produces no warnings.
+24. Error rows do not stop the import. All other contact rows are still processed.
+
+### Creating and updating
+
+25. An applied row whose email is not in the repository creates a contact with the
+    row's name, phone (if valid and non-blank) and tags. Its normalised email is
+    appended to `created`.
+26. An applied row whose email matches an existing contact (case-insensitive,
+    whitespace-trimmed; ` ANN@X.COM` matches `ann@x.com`) updates that contact and
+    never creates a second one. Its normalised email is appended to `updated`. This
+    happens even if nothing actually changed.
+27. On update:
+    - the name is replaced by the file's name;
+    - the phone is replaced only if the cell holds a valid phone, so a blank cell,
+      missing column or invalid phone keeps the existing phone;
+    - the file's tags are added to the existing tags, and existing tags are never
+      removed;
+    - the email (identity) stays the same normalised value.
+28. Contacts in the repository that are not mentioned in the file are untouched.
+29. All values stored by the import are normalised in the same way as values stored
+    through `Contact.create`.
+
+### Report
+
+30. `created` and `updated` contain normalised emails, in file order. An email
+    appears at most once across both lists.
+31. `errors` and `warnings` are in row order. Every `RowIssue.row` is the row number
+    defined above.
+32. `RowIssue.column` is the lower-case logical column name: `name`, `email`,
+    `phone` or `tags`. In a `first_name`/`last_name` file, name problems use
+    `name`. It may be `None` only for issues that concern the whole row.
+33. `RowIssue.message` is a short, human-readable reason.
+34. `ImportFileError` derives from `ContactError` and carries a human-readable
+    message.
+
+### Round trip
+
+35. Importing `export_csv(contacts)` into an empty repository recreates contacts
+    equal to the originals. Importing it into the repository it was exported from
+    puts every email in `updated`, reports no errors or warnings, and leaves every
+    contact equal to what it was.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Delimiters other than comma, including detecting `;`-delimited files.
+- Encodings other than UTF-8, such as Windows-125x or UTF-16.
+- A byte-size limit. Only the 1000-contact-row limit applies.
+- Deleting contacts, removing tags, clearing phones or changing a contact's email
+  through import.
+- A dry-run or preview mode, and all-or-nothing imports when only some rows are bad.
+- A CLI command or HTTP endpoint. The HTTP API lives in another package. Exposing
+  the importer through `ContactBook` or the package `__init__` is optional.
+- Thread safety, which the repository doesn't offer either.
